1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,094
AGBE. Àgbẹ̀ means farmer in Yoruba.

2
00:00:02,094 --> 00:00:07,000
A farming advisor that runs on an ordinary laptop,
with the internet switched off.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,518
Nigeria has about one agricultural extension officer
for every few thousand farming households.

4
00:00:10,518 --> 00:00:13,703
The knowledge that would raise a smallholder's yield
is not secret, and it is not new.

5
00:00:13,703 --> 00:00:16,000
It sits in extension manuals.
It just never reaches the field.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,750
So we asked it what farmers actually ask.

7
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:21,635
Holes in the young maize leaves, and something like
wet sawdust in the centre of the plant.

8
00:00:21,635 --> 00:00:22,574
It says fall armyworm.

9
00:00:22,574 --> 00:00:27,142
Then it tells you how to check before you spend money:
a pale upside down Y on the head of the caterpillar,

10
00:00:27,142 --> 00:00:28,550
and four dark dots near the tail.

11
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:30,599
That is real fall armyworm biology, not a guess.

12
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:32,341
This is the part that matters.

13
00:00:32,341 --> 00:00:35,070
Look at the status bar. That is aeroplane mode.

14
00:00:35,070 --> 00:00:39,600
No internet, no API key, no account, no data cost.
The model is on the laptop.

15
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:41,575
Now something it should not answer.

16
00:00:41,575 --> 00:00:43,494
A child with a fever and vomiting.

17
00:00:43,494 --> 00:00:46,486
It declines. It says it does not give medical advice,

18
00:00:46,486 --> 00:00:52,300
tells you to contact a doctor today, and not to give
any medication without speaking to a professional.

19
00:00:52,299 --> 00:00:54,270
Getting a one billion parameter model to say no
took four attempts.

20
00:00:54,270 --> 00:00:57,299
For a tool used by people with no alternative,
knowing where it stops matters as much as what it knows.

21
00:00:57,299 --> 00:01:00,222
The challenge publishes its scoring formula,
so we read it before writing any code.

22
00:01:00,222 --> 00:01:03,285
Throughput above fifteen tokens a second earns nothing,
and memory is charged linearly.

23
00:01:03,285 --> 00:01:06,912
That makes the obvious move, running the biggest model
that fits in eight gigabytes, exactly backwards.

24
00:01:06,912 --> 00:01:10,257
The three billion model gives up fourteen and a half points
before answering a single question.

25
00:01:10,257 --> 00:01:12,300
So we measured five candidates, and built the one billion.

26
00:01:12,299 --> 00:01:16,059
Eight hundred and fourteen megabytes on disk.
Under a gigabyte of memory.

27
00:01:16,059 --> 00:01:19,304
Twenty tokens a second on four CPU threads,
with no GPU at all.

28
00:01:19,304 --> 00:01:24,299
Forty seven and a half of the fifty available
engineering points, measured rather than estimated.

29
00:01:24,299 --> 00:01:28,349
Downloaded once. After that it works
with the cable pulled out.

30
00:01:28,349 --> 00:01:33,299
The weights are public, so you can check
every number in this video yourself.

